    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d316">

                <pb facs="0605" n="535" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg535"/>

                <head>No. 315.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Davis to Mr. Schuyler.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-01-22"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 22,
                        1883</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 17.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to a previous instruction, No.
                    15, of the 5th instant. I herewith inclose a copy of a dispatch (No. 158, of the
                    25th ultimo),<anchor n="†" xml:id="d316-f1"/> from Mr. Wallace, our minister at
                    Constantinople, in which he details <pb facs="0606" n="536" xml:id="pg536"/> the
                    manner in which he was enabled to gain the release of eleven Greeks who were
                    imprisoned for political offenses at Constantinople. Mr. Wallace also incloses a
                    letter from Athens thanking him for his successful efforts in behalf of the
                    prisoners.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JOHN DAVIS,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Acting
                        Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d316-f1" n="†" xml:id="d316-f1-content">
                <p>See Mr. Wallace to Mr. Frelinghuysen, No. 158, December 25, 1882, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">post.</hi></p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
